---
import Base from '../../layouts/Base.astro';
const b = import.meta.env.BASE_URL.replace(/\/$/, '');
const repo = import.meta.env.PUBLIC_RETO_REPO_URL || '#';
---
<Base lang="en" title="Take-home task — TailorMind">
  <h1>Take-home task</h1>
  <p class="lead">A platformer in Lua. Thirty to forty-five minutes, with the requirements arriving while you work.</p>

  <h2>It does not all fit, and that is deliberate</h2>
  <p>More will land than can be done well in that time, and some of it will contradict what you were asked earlier. Attempting everything and shipping a broken game is the worst possible outcome.</p>
  <p>We want the opposite: <strong>the most working software, in the least time, with the sacrifices said out loud</strong>. Three finished, playable things beat seven half-done ones.</p>

  <h2>Required</h2>
  <p>This take-home is <strong>required</strong>. Without the repo URL in the form, the application does not move to the interview.</p>
  <p><strong>Review what you ship.</strong> Lean on any LLM you want — that is part of the exercise — but the deliverable carries your name. If it includes content outside the spec, we read that as no final review.</p>

  <h2>When to do it</h2>
  <p>Submitting is required; the timing is yours. The take-home is independent of CV screening. You can do it now or wait until we've screened your CV and ask for it explicitly (~5 business days). Both paths are equally valid. <strong>If we reject on CV we won't evaluate the take-home</strong> — but we'll tell you within ~5 days so you don't keep wondering.</p>

  <h2>How it works</h2>
  <ol>
    <li><strong>Beforehand, off the clock:</strong> install <a href="https://love2d.org">LÖVE 11.4</a>, clone the harness, open it with <code>love .</code> and run its tests with <code>love . --test</code>. It ships a character that runs and jumps, and five passing tests. Take as long as you like: none of this counts.</li>
    <li><strong>Practise on the demo requirements.</strong> The repo carries three, in exactly the shape the real ones take. They do not count either.</li>
    <li><strong>Open your run</strong> with <code>love . --run YOU@EMAIL</code>. That is when the clock starts.</li>
    <li><strong>Requirements arrive</strong> in the game's panel, one every few minutes. All of them land inside the first 28, so whether you stop at 30 or at 45 you see yours in full. They are not in the repo, cannot be fetched early, and there is no way to ask for more.</li>
  </ol>

  <h2>Why the task is not in the repo</h2>
  <p>The repo is only the harness: a working start, the tests and the panel. The requirements are drawn per candidate and downloaded when you open your run.</p>
  <p>The reason is fairness: <strong>cloning it today is worth exactly what cloning it last week would have been</strong>. If the task were published, whoever read it first would have more time than everyone else, and we would stop measuring the same thing in all of you. Set up your environment as much as you want; what we grade starts when you open the run.</p>

  <h2>Stack</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>LÖVE (love2d) 11.4+</strong> and <strong>Lua</strong>. Nothing else to install: no luarocks, no dependencies, no build step.</li>
    <li>Game logic lives in plain Lua with no <code>love.*</code> calls, which is why the suite runs in half a second without opening a window. You may break that separation — but then say so, and say why.</li>
    <li>Any LLM you like, as much as you like. We hire you to use it.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>What you ship</h2>
  <ol>
    <li><strong>A game that runs.</strong> <code>love .</code> has to start and be playable. Test it from a clean clone before sending: "it doesn't start" is more common than you'd think, and then there is nothing to look at.</li>
    <li><strong><code>SUPUESTOS.md</code></strong> — one line per decision you had to make because the requirement did not define it, and at the end, what you left out and why. This file is the document, not the appendix.</li>
    <li><strong><code>REQUISITOS-RECIBIDOS.md</code></strong>, exactly as the game wrote it. Don't edit it: it is your copy of what you drew and ours of what we grade against.</li>
    <li>Your tests, if you wrote any. <code>love . --test</code> should still be green.</li>
    <li>Reopen your application form (with the edit link from your confirmation email) and paste the repo URL.</li>
  </ol>

  <h2>What we assess</h2>
  <p>In this order, and the order matters:</p>
  <ol>
    <li><strong>What you left out, and whether you said so.</strong> Doing it all is not an option. Choosing is, and choosing well shows.</li>
    <li><strong>The assumptions.</strong> Some requirement will leave something undefined. Filling the gap in silence is never the good answer.</li>
    <li><strong>That it starts and plays.</strong> No ceremony.</li>
    <li><strong>The code</strong>, last. If it reads and the tests pass, it is fine. We are not looking for astronaut architecture in half an hour.</li>
  </ol>

  <h2>Rules</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>There is nobody to ask while the clock runs: when something is undefined, <strong>write the assumption</strong> and move on.</li>
    <li>Use any AI you want. <strong>Using it costs you nothing; shipping it unread does.</strong> If the model gave you something you did not verify, say so in <code>SUPUESTOS.md</code> — that costs you nothing either.</li>
    <li>Commits carry timestamps. No need to police yourself: it shows on its own.</li>
  </ul>

  {repo !== '#'
    ? <p class="not-prose"><a href={repo} class="inline-block px-4 py-2 rounded bg-[color:var(--color-brand-600)] text-white">Clone the harness</a></p>
    : <p class="not-prose p-4 bg-amber-50 border border-amber-300 rounded">Challenge repo URL not configured yet. Set <code>PUBLIC_RETO_REPO_URL</code>.</p>}

  <p><a href={`${b}/en/apply/`}>Start by submitting the application form</a></p>
</Base>
